# Citation metadata. GitHub renders a "Cite this repository" button from this file.
#
# TODO(owner), both require action outside the repository:
#   1. ORCID — add `orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0000-0000-0000"` under the author
#      once registered (free, https://orcid.org/register). It is what links this
#      software to the rest of your publication record, and JOSS requires one.
#   2. DOI — after linking the repo on https://zenodo.org and cutting one release,
#      Zenodo mints a concept DOI covering all versions. Add it under `identifiers`
#      and to the README badge row. `.zenodo.json` already carries the metadata.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use NovaFabric in your research or tooling, please cite it as below."
title: "NovaFabric: portable, verifiable execution evidence for AI and HPC runs"
abstract: >-
  NovaFabric is a self-hosted, open-source toolkit that captures, replays,
  diffs, and audits AI agent and model runs as portable, secret-redacted,
  signed evidence capsules. It runs in the user's own infrastructure, from a
  laptop to a cluster, with no accounts and no telemetry. It is built around
  five primitives — Asset Registry, Run Capsule, Replay, Lineage, and Evidence
  Bundle — and four replay modes (forensic, mocked, semantic, exact). Evidence
  bundles verify offline with only sha256sum and an ed25519 verifier, requiring
  no NovaFabric installation. Capture works without application code changes and
  interoperates with OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions, in-toto/DSSE,
  RFC 3161, W3C PROV, OpenLineage, and CycloneDX.
type: software
authors:
